    "body": "@annevk\r\n\r\nI was thinking a solution could be done the same way Google created an attribute for lazy loading by creating the `loading` attribute.\r\n\r\nIn this case have an attribute like:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<table table=\"responsive\">\r\n..\r\n</table>\r\n\r\nor\r\n\r\n<table responsive>\r\n..\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n(Same like checkbox's being checked)\r\n\r\n<input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"vehicle\" value=\"Car\" checked>\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Yah, I guess this is a rendering issue.**\r\n\r\nI was thinking on these kind of lines of thought, say you have a table like this:\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/55155131/64908233-42597000-d6f5-11e9-8ffb-22a4e5dc57d3.png)\r\n\r\nBut there are 100 rows and you wanted to get to row 1 and column 100, you would have to scroll down the web page move the slider right, then scroll back up the web page, to get to the correct table panel. Not a nice user experience!\r\n\r\nInstead, I was thinking browser vendors could have a more repsonsive solution by allowing some kind of sliding action, e.g.\r\n\r\n![ezgif-2-d4e92cd5e880](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/55155131/64908330-8305b900-d6f6-11e9-8e7f-2fec1dd16ae2.gif)\r\n\r\nIn the above example it's just showing a single row, but the same functionality could move the whole table left and right.\r\n\r\nTo turn this feature on and off, you would have an attribute for the table element.\r\n\r\nHope that makes a bit more sense.",
